# Antiques Marketplace architecture

WordPress theme that reads a remote Catawiki MySQL catalog (`products`). Prices are stored in **EUR**.

The table is one row per Catawiki lot (~29k). PRIMARY KEY is autoincrement `id`; Catawiki lot id is `product_id` with **UNIQUE** `uniq_product_id` so the scraper `ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE` can upsert. Listing/dashboard SQL still uses `antiques_marketplace_products_from_latest_lot()` / `antiques_marketplace_latest_lot_where_sql()` as a safety net. Do not PHP-dedupe thousands of rows.

## Where code belongs

- `inc/database.php` — shared `wpdb` connection, schema/index cache
- `inc/catalog.php` — listing SQL (paginate in SQL, never pull thousands of rows)
- `inc/currency.php` — EUR → USD (English) / JPY (Japanese) via ECB Frankfurter
- `inc/categories.php` — 10 taxonomy sections + hashtag URLs
- `inc/bids.php` — `bidding_history` JSON (winning bidder country)
- `inc/dashboard.php` — market-trend equations
- Templates: `front-page.php` (public marketing landing), `page-listings.php` + `template-parts/catalog-listings.php` (gated catalog at `/data/`), `single-external-product.php`, `page-dashboard.php`

The homepage is a public landing. Catalog search, filters, and category URLs must use `antiques_marketplace_listings_url()`, never `home_url('/')`. Do not run catalog COUNT/dashboard aggregations on `front-page.php`.

## Search

Use FULLTEXT `MATCH(product_title) AGAINST (... IN BOOLEAN MODE)` when available. Search title only. Do not `LIKE` description. Do not PHP-dedupe 5k rows.

## UI currency

Format with `antiques_marketplace_format_display_price()`. Convert min/max filters back to EUR before SQL. Always show the ECB rate caption.

## Categories

Use the 10 slugs in `antiques_marketplace_catalog_taxonomy()`. Hashtags link to `/data/?category={slug}`. Do not invent a 11th section unless the taxonomy map is updated.

## Source listing

Keep `antiques_marketplace_show_source_listing()` false until product asks to restore it.
